  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nWhile high pressure will start to settle into the region tonight, \nthe center will be only be near the Ontario/Quebec border by early \nSaturday morning. Thus the pressure gradient may not relax quite a \nbit as it appeared in previous days. Therefore, northwest winds may \nremain on the breezier side through the night which may help to keep \ntemperatures from plummeting. Some high clouds may also be floating \naround. Still, it appears that we will see some lows in the mid to \nupper 30s across the northern mountains which could lead to some \nareas of frost. Lows will mainly be in the low to mid 40s elsewhere. \nThe most favorable area for frost appears to be over the far \nnorthern valleys where we will likely see winds taper off sooner. \nHave held off on any Frost Advisory for now given the uncertainty \nregarding winds and cloud cover. \n\nThe core of the high will move into New England on Saturday which \nwill mean fair weather with high temperatures mainly in the 60s, \nplenty of sunshine, and light/variable winds. Lows on Saturday night \nshould be similar to tonight but maybe just a touch warmer with \nclouds forecast to be on the increase through the night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nConvection that has yet to blossom over the central Plains is \nmodeled to produce a mid level short wave that will track west to \neast across the Northeast late Sunday. As is often the case with \nconvectively induced short waves, there is a spread amongst model \nguidance in the trajectory and strength of the wave which has \nimplications for PoPs and QPF amounts. There will be additional \nsupport for lift Sunday into Sunday night as the right entrance \nregion of an upper jet slides over northern New England. Models \ngenerally agree that this wave will produce a surface low \ntracking near the area to bring some beneficial rainfall \ncentered on Sunday afternoon. The latest NBM brings at least \nchances PoPs across the entire area with precip chances 60-80 \npercent across NH into SW Maine by Sunday afternoon. Total QPF \nwill range from 0.75 inches across SW NH to around a tenth of an\ninch across central Somerset County. Although some ensemble \nmembers and the RRFS leave the forecast area mostly dry, I am \nnot inclined to deviate from the NBM based on the suite of \nguidance. PoPs will decrease from NW to SE as the surface low \nexits and high pressure builds in from the NW.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nDeterministic and ensemble guidance are in good agreement that broad \nhigh pressure will drift from southern Canada across northern New \nEngland Monday through Wednesday. This will promote fair weather \nwith temperatures running below normal. There is a signal for a \ncoastal low to develop near the Mid Atlantic during this time range \nthat could throw some clouds into the area and hamper radiational \ncooling. If skies remain clear at night than temperatures will \nlikely drop low enough for frost formation across interior\nvalleys. Models suggest the coastal low will slowly drift \nnorthward the second half of next week with precip chances \narriving around Thursday.",
